// ADR 0025 R-A — THE REFUSE HALF: MUTATING THE OWNER UNDER A LIVE `SliceStream`.
//
// `SliceStream<R>` is `#[borrow_carrying]` (`stdlib/lang/stream/stream.logos`):
// it holds the caller's `&[R]` and copies nothing, which is exactly what makes
// it free and exactly what makes this program wrong. Growing the owning `Vec`
// while the stream is live can move the buffer, and every batch the stream has
// handed out — or is about to — would point at freed storage. The emitted scan
// puts the wrap and the pull in one generated function, so the shape a USER can
// reach is this one: the stream and the owner in the same scope.
//
// ⚠ THE POINT IS THAT THIS IS A TYPE FACT, NOT A CONVENTION. Rung 1 of ADR 0025
// §7's pin ladder is "nothing to pin": no counter, no runtime check, no comment
// asking the caller to be careful — the borrow checker refuses it, and it
// refuses it because `SliceStream` BORROWS rather than owning. `Buffer<R>`
// (`logos.mem.stream`), which OWNS its rows, accepts the analogous program;
// that asymmetry IS the seam between the two types.
//
// PAIRED WITH THE ADMIT HALF at the bottom of
// `pass/stream_slice_stream_seam` — the SAME `push` on the SAME `Vec`, after
// the stream's last use, compiles and its row is then visible. A refusal
// fixture alone cannot tell separation from over-refusal: a checker that
// refused `push` anywhere near a stream would pass this file and fail that one.
package slice_stream_mutate_under_scan_fail;

use logos.lang.option;
use logos.lang.stream;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut rows: Vec<i64> = Vec::<i64>::new();
    rows.push(10i64);
    rows.push(20i64);

    let mut s: SliceStream<i64> = SliceStream::<i64>::new(rows.as_slice());

    // REFUSED HERE: `rows` is borrowed by `s`, and `s` is used below.
    rows.push(30i64);

    let ob: Option<&[i64]> = s.next_batch();
    if ob.is_none() { return 1i32; }
    let b: &[i64] = ob.unwrap();
    if b.len() != 3i64 { return 2i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
